  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "The product of all real roots of the equation \\(x^{\\log_{10}x}=10\\) is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(10\\)",
    "D": "\\(10^{-1}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The logarithm in the equation requires \\(x>0\\). Taking a base-ten logarithm is useful because it brings down the exponent, producing another factor of \\(\\log_{10}x\\):\n\\[\n\\log_{10}\\!\\left(x^{\\log_{10}x}\\right)\n=(\\log_{10}x)^2=\\log_{10}10=1.\n\\]\nA real number whose square is \\(1\\) is either \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\log_{10}x=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\log_{10}x=-1,\n\\]\nso \\(x=10\\) or \\(x=1/10\\).\n\nBoth candidates satisfy the original equation: \\(10^1=10\\) and \\((1/10)^{-1}=10\\). Every root must be one of these candidates, so their product is\n\\[\n10\\cdot\\frac1{10}=\\boxed{1},\n\\]\nchoice A.",
